==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|guiser}}. Compare also {{cog|nds-de|Kieser|t=an obstinate person; brute; savage}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|/ˈɡizɚ/|lang=en}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈɡiːzə/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-au-geezer.ogg|Audio (AU)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|iːzə(ɹ)|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|geyser|Giza}} (in some dialects)

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal|chiefly|British|dated in US}} A [[male]] person.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:man}}
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=1922|author={{w|P. G. Wodehouse}}|title=Right Ho, Jeeves|chapter=19|passage=You are a silly young '''geezer'''.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|chiefly|Cockney|slang}} Someone affable but morally dubious; a [[wide boy]].
#: {{syn|en|spiv|wide boy}}
#* '''2003''', Carlton Leach, ''Muscle'', John Blake Publishing {{ISBN|9781784184728}}
#*: He turned out to be a proper '''geezer''' who was willing to listen to my proposition that if he took the door at the Ministry, I would pay him £400 a month to mark my cards.
#* '''2009''', Dreda Say Mitchell, ''Geezer Girls'', Hachette UK {{ISBN|9781848946163}}
#*: He was a bit of a '''geezer'''. Used to box with the Krays when he was a young &#39;un.
#* '''2013''', Charlotte Ward, ''Why Am I Always the One Before 'The One'?'', Hachette UK {{ISBN|9780755364800}}
#*: When I&#39;d first met Adam, at work when we were both 23, the fact that he seemed a little rough around the edges appealed to me. He was a bit of a '''geezer''', a joker, one of the lads.
# {{lb|en|UK|slang}} Informal address to a [[male]].
#: {{syn|en|mate|Thesaurus:friend}}
#: {{ux|en|Hi '''geezer''', you alright?}}
# {{lb|en|informal|chiefly|US|sometimes|mildly|derogatory}} An [[old]] person, usually a male, typically a cranky old man.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:old man}}
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=1885|author=Corin|title=The Truth about the Stage|passage=In the right-hand division lay the two old '''geezers''', as Sandy styled the landlord and his wife.}}
#* '''2000''', Moira McDonald, "[http://community.seattletimes.nwsource.com/archive/?date=20000825&slug=4038797 Outtakes]," ''Seattle Times'', 25 Aug. (retrieved 6 Sep. 2008):
#*: The technical term for a female '''geezer''' is "old broad," but this is irrelevant, as nobody in Hollywood makes films about women over 55.
#* '''2014''', The Geezer Gallery, "[https://web.archive.org/web/20140428111606/http://www.geezergallery.com/content/why-geezer]," (retrieved 31 Jan 2014):
#*: Why Geezer? Why would a fine arts gallery choose a name that conjures images of a grumpy old guy sitting on the front porch hollering, “get off my lawn”?
# {{lb|en|British}} A device for [[boil]]ing water for such [[domestic]] uses as heating or washing; a [[boiler]]. The normal spelling is water {{m|en|geyser}}.
# {{lb|en|archaic|British|slang}} [[wife|Wife]]; [[old]] [[woman]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:wife|Thesaurus:old woman}}
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=1882|author=J. F. Mitchell|title=Jimmy Johnson's Holiday|passage=He'd flirt and boat, but never wrote / A note to his old '''geezer'''.}}
#* '''1886''', ''Her Mother's Got the Hump'':
#*: This frizzle-headed old '''geezer''' had a chin on her as rough well, as rough as her family, and they're rough 'uns.

